components.responsibility:
Four helpers.

(a) Walked(ScanStore) returns a bool[]. It walks from the root over FirstChild/ChildCount with an explicit Stack (RollupOracle.CheckStructure's idiom), then runs a reverse pass. An entry is true if it reads NotListed or any registered child is true. It looks at every child, so it shares no logic with the chain read.

(b) RaggedTree() builds a small real NTFS tree, about 15 folders, of ragged depth. Shapes it must contain:
- a folder whose first subfolder is 3 deep and whose last subfolder is empty (a: a1\a11\a111\leaf.txt, a2);
- a folder whose last subfolder is the deepest, 4 levels (c: c1\c11\x.txt, c2\c21\c211\c2111\y.txt);
- an empty folder (b);
- a last folder that holds only files (e);
- blocks that end in files, and one block that ends in a junction to TempTree.Outside after its last subfolder. The backward scan has to skip both.
The ragged depth is required: spike 1 measured 0 queue mismatches on a uniform 3-ary tree.

(c) RaggedOrder is the expected folder listing preorder. It is asserted equal to the baseline's FolderEnds, exactly as SweepOrder is. This pins the order, including empty folders, and it fails loudly if NTFS orders the names differently.

(d) AssertListedDepthFirst(ScanStore) walks preorder with children in block order. It requires the FirstChild of every folder with ChildCount > 0 to strictly increase. That holds for every prefix of a depth-first registration. It fires on queue, first-first or interleaved order whenever two non-empty folders are registered out of preorder.

proposals.note:
The only proposal. Taken as the core. Adjustments:
- the NotListedTests helpers it needs are private, so they are made internal, not SweepTree alone;
- a listing-order pin is added, because the FirstChild pin skips folders listed empty;
- an interleave feeder is added for 'made parallel';
- the allocation assert is a bound, not exactly 0;
- one Kind-byte architecture rule is added;
- RaggedTree gets a junction after a last subfolder.
